    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "[auto-routed: surprise=1.00] Implement the stochastic Fourier mode hypothesis via a PyTorch workflow. Generate target functions as superpositions of M random Fourier modes with frequencies in [f_min, f_max]. For each constraint count w ∈ {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}, scan hidden layer widths h ∈ {4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32} to find the minimum width N_c(w) achieving zero violation (max absolute error ≤ 1e-3 on a held-out set). Average over 3 random seeds for each (w, M) pair to address the \"con\" of stochastic variance. Compare N_c(w) scaling against the rigid polynomial baseline from iter 145 to test the fatness prediction (α ≥ 0.5 vs. bounded rigidity)."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "(none)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "For the stochastic target, N_c(w) will grow super-linearly with w (e.g., N_c(10)≈8, N_c(50)≈32 or higher), indicating fatness (α ≥ 0.5), whereas the rigid polynomial baseline from iter 145 will plateau at N_c ≤ 12 across all w. Training and validation errors will remain ≤ 1e-3 for widths at or above N_c(w), with stochastic realizations showing seed-to-seed variation in N_c(w) of < 2."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "prediction_inputs"
    },
    {
